## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
For every ordinal \(\alpha > \omega\), does \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\) hold if and only if \(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
Yes, for every ordinal \(\alpha > \omega\), \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\) if and only if \(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\).  

**Proof**:  
1. **Forward Direction (\(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right| \implies \alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\))**:  
   - By definition, \(V_{\alpha}\) is the cumulative hierarchy up to \(\alpha\), and \(\left|V_{\alpha}\right| = \beth_{\alpha}\) when \(\alpha > \omega\).  
   - The constructible hierarchy \(L_{\alpha}\) satisfies \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = |\alpha|\) for \(\alpha > \omega\) (by Lemma 23.25 in the reference document).  
   - If \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\), then \(|\alpha| = \beth_{\alpha}\). Since \(\alpha\) is an ordinal, this implies \(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\).  

2. **Reverse Direction (\(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha} \implies \left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\))**:  
   - If \(\alpha = \beth_{\alpha}\), then \(\left|V_{\alpha}\right| = \beth_{\alpha} = \alpha\).  
   - As above, \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = |\alpha|\), so \(\left|L_{\alpha}\right| = \left|V_{\alpha}\right|\).  

Thus, the equivalence holds. \(\boxed{\text{Yes}}\)
</GROUND_TRUTH>

